s' War", "context": "The treaty established the principle of sovereignty, cementing the Dutch Republic's independence."}, {"sourceSlug": "rembrandt", "sourceName": "Rembrandt", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "dutch-golden-age", "targetName": "Dutch Golden Age", "context": "He was one of the prominent figures who made lasting contributions to the world of art during the Dutch Golden Age."}], "places": [{"name": "Low Countries, Netherlands", "role": "Conflict zone"}, {"name": "Leiden, Netherlands", "role": "Siege site"}, {"name": "Heiligerlee, Netherlands", "role": "Battle site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Eighty Years' War", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "eighty-years-war"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q164432"], "tags": ["Seventeen Provinces", "civil war", "Civil Wars & Internal Conflicts", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Eighty Years' War left a notable mark on Early Modern history, documented across 74 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Divided and reconstructed political order. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Eighty Years' War played a pivotal role in shaping the modern world by establishing the principle of sovereignty, emphasizing self-determination and individual rights, and influencing the course of European and global history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-08T06:24:40Z", "editorId": "Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct·cloud·GH#27119753363", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "16th- and 17th-century dutch revolt against the habsburgs Took place in Low Countries. Date: 1568-06-02. Ended: 1648-01-30.", "newValue": "The Eighty Years' War (1568-1648) was a pivotal rebellion of the Dutch people against the Habsburg dynasty, unfolding in the Low Countries. This cataclysmic conflict, sparked by the Dutch Revolt, took place over a century, pitting the Protestant Netherlands against the Catholic Habsburgs, in a struggle for independence and self-governance. The war's extensive impact would be felt across the continent, foreshadowing the broader European struggles that would eventually lead to the Thirty Years' War and the Peace of Westphalia.

The Eighty Years' War comprised multiple phases, each marked by significant events and turning points. The first phase saw the Dutch rebels, led by William the Silent, engage in guerrilla warfare against the Spanish Empire's forces, with the iconic Battle of Heiligerlee (1568) marking a key early victory for the Dutch. The siege of Leiden (1573-1574) showcased the resourcefulness and resilience of the Dutch, while the Synod of Dort (1618-1619) exemplified the theological divisions that would continue to shape the conflict. The Dutch West India Company's establishment in 1621 marked a turning point, as the Dutch began to assert their maritime and colonial dominance.

